---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// type: "reel" uses /reel/{id}/embed/ and a 9:16 frame with chrome cropped.
// type: "post" uses /p/{id}/embed/ and a taller frame to fit the photo plus chrome.
const posts = [
  { id: "DXKTjK-RaCf", type: "reel" as const },
  { id: "DXcYo5viZgU", type: "reel" as const },
  { id: "DXuZu4wkfwi", type: "post" as const },
];
---

<Base title="Social Media | Grace Joy Park">
  <h1>Social Media</h1>

  <section class="reflection">
    <p>
      Over the course of the semester, my Instagram (@grace.park_uga)
      became a place where I could practice journalism outside the
      classroom and document what I was learning in Reporting I.
    </p>
    <p>
      Honestly, when we started, I was not comfortable with the idea of
      building a public-facing account tied to my name and my work.
      Posting my own reporting felt exposing, and I worried about how it
      would come across. Pushing through that hesitation ended up being
      one of the most valuable parts of the semester. I had to step out
      of my comfort zone, and in doing so I learned what it actually
      means to maintain a professional social media presence as a
      journalist. That meant thinking carefully about tone, being
      consistent with how I introduced myself and my stories, writing
      captions that sounded credible rather than casual and treating
      every post as something a future editor or source could see.
    </p>
    <p>
      One of the biggest skills I picked up was producing short-form
      video. I learned how to cover an event in under 60 seconds,
      figuring out what to include, writing a tight script, choosing
      B-roll that actually supports the story and adding captions so the
      reel reads even without sound. Editing for that length forced me
      to think harder about what the lede really is, and that carries
      over into my written reporting.
    </p>
    <p>
      Following accounts like @nytimes, @apnews and @theredandblack also
      shaped how I approach my own posts. The Red and Black in particular
      gave me a closer look at AP Style in practice: how headlines get
      phrased, when numbers are spelled out versus written as digits and
      how attributions are handled in caption copy. Seeing AP Style
      applied to local UGA stories made it click for me in a way the
      stylebook on its own didn&rsquo;t. The bigger national accounts
      taught me a different lesson about how a major newsroom packages
      breaking news for a scrolling audience without losing accuracy,
      and watching how they balanced personality with credibility gave
      me a model for what a professional journalist&rsquo;s feed can
      look like.
    </p>
    <p>
      In terms of engagement, I ended the semester with 54 followers,
      and my reels consistently pulled between 400 and 500 views each,
      which felt like a strong sign that the content was reaching beyond
      just my immediate circle. My semester wrap-up post pulling together
      everything I worked on in Reporting I was a useful way to see which
      formats my audience responded to most.
    </p>
    <p>
      What I take away most is how much Instagram has opened up for
      journalists, and how much I personally grew by leaning into it
      instead of avoiding it. It isn&rsquo;t just a place to repost
      articles. Reels reach people who would never click through to a
      website, story polls and question stickers can crowdsource sources
      or quotes, and the platform doubles as a live portfolio that
      editors and recruiters can find without me having to send
      anything. For a student journalist, a public Instagram is
      essentially a clip file that&rsquo;s always growing, and I leave
      this semester a lot more confident about putting my work out there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    {posts.map(({ id, type }) => (
      <div class={`embed ${type === "reel" ? "is-reel" : "is-post"}`}>
        <iframe
          src={`https://www.instagram.com/${type === "reel" ? "reel" : "p"}/${id}/embed/`}
          title={type === "reel" ? "Instagram reel" : "Instagram post"}
          loading="lazy"
          scrolling="no"
          allow="autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture; web-share"
          allowtransparency="true"
          allowfullscreen
        ></iframe>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .reflection {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .reflection p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .reflection p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(320px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: start;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .embed {
    width: 100%;
    /*
     * Cap each embed at the width Instagram's internal player actually
     * fills. Above ~330px the video stops scaling and IG fills the leftover
     * space with black side bars. Capping here removes the bars and keeps
     * the embed centered inside its grid column.
     */
    max-width: 330px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    background: #000;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    overflow: hidden;
    position: relative;
  }

  /*
   * Reels: 9:16 frame so the reel video fills top to bottom. Then oversize
   * the iframe and offset it upward so Instagram's username header and
   * likes/comments footer push past the visible edges.
   */
  .embed.is-reel {
    aspect-ratio: 9 / 16;
  }

  .embed.is-reel iframe {
    position: absolute;
    top: -60px;
    left: 0;
    width: 100% !important;
    height: calc(100% + 220px) !important;
    border: 0;
    display: block;
  }

  /*
   * Carousels / photo posts: photo inside is roughly square, so a 1:1
   * frame plus the chrome-crop trick shows just the image.
   */
  .embed.is-post {
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    background: #fff;
  }

  .embed.is-post iframe {
    position: absolute;
    top: -60px;
    left: 0;
    width: 100% !important;
    height: calc(100% + 220px) !important;
    border: 0;
    display: block;
  }
</style>
